WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:03.700 --> 00:00:05.670
When observing the
natural world, many of us

00:00:05.670 --> 00:00:07.610
notice a somewhat
beautiful dichotomy.

00:00:10.170 --> 00:00:12.470
No two things are
ever exactly alike,

00:00:12.470 --> 00:00:16.370
but they all seem to follow
some underlying form.

00:00:16.370 --> 00:00:19.870
And Plato believed that the
true forms of the universe

00:00:19.870 --> 00:00:21.440
were hidden from us.

00:00:21.440 --> 00:00:23.390
Through observation
of the natural world,

00:00:23.390 --> 00:00:27.550
we could merely acquire
approximate knowledge of them.

00:00:27.550 --> 00:00:30.080
They were hidden blueprints.

00:00:30.080 --> 00:00:31.960
The pure forms were
only accessible

00:00:31.960 --> 00:00:35.060
through abstract reasoning of
philosophy and mathematics.

00:00:35.060 --> 00:00:37.210
For example, the circle.

00:00:37.210 --> 00:00:39.990
He describes as that
which has the distance

00:00:39.990 --> 00:00:44.090
from its circumference to
its center everywhere equal.

00:00:44.090 --> 00:00:47.090
Yet we will never find
a material manifestation

00:00:47.090 --> 00:00:51.000
of a perfect circle, or a
perfectly straight line.

00:00:51.000 --> 00:00:52.960
Though interestingly,
Plato speculated

00:00:52.960 --> 00:00:55.710
that after an uncountable
number of years,

00:00:55.710 --> 00:00:58.360
the universe will
reach an ideal state,

00:00:58.360 --> 00:01:01.500
returning to its perfect form.

00:01:01.500 --> 00:01:05.010
This platonic focus on
an abstract pure forms

00:01:05.010 --> 00:01:07.380
remained popular for centuries.

00:01:07.380 --> 00:01:09.840
And it wasn't until
the 16th century,

00:01:09.840 --> 00:01:13.780
when people tried to
embrace the messy variation

00:01:13.780 --> 00:01:17.660
in the real world, and apply
mathematics to tease out

00:01:17.660 --> 00:01:18.760
underlying patterns.

00:01:22.300 --> 00:01:26.190
Bernoulli refined the
idea of expectation.

00:01:26.190 --> 00:01:29.380
He was focused on a method
of accurately estimating

00:01:29.380 --> 00:01:32.210
the unknown probability
of some event,

00:01:32.210 --> 00:01:35.540
based on the number of
times the event occurs

00:01:35.540 --> 00:01:37.350
in independent trials.

00:01:37.350 --> 00:01:39.720
And he uses a simple example.

00:01:39.720 --> 00:01:42.140
Suppose that without
your knowledge

00:01:42.140 --> 00:01:45.420
3,000 light pebbles
and 2,000 dark pebbles

00:01:45.420 --> 00:01:47.220
are hidden in an urn.

00:01:47.220 --> 00:01:50.770
And that, to determine the
ratio of white versus black

00:01:50.770 --> 00:01:53.510
by experiment, you
draw one pebble

00:01:53.510 --> 00:01:58.030
after another, with replacement,
and note how many times

00:01:58.030 --> 00:02:01.980
a white pebble is
drawn versus black.

00:02:01.980 --> 00:02:05.230
He went on to prove that
the expected value of white

00:02:05.230 --> 00:02:09.660
versus black observations will
converge on the actual ratio,

00:02:09.660 --> 00:02:12.350
as the number of
trials increases.

00:02:12.350 --> 00:02:14.840
Known as the Weak
Law of Large Numbers.

00:02:17.670 --> 00:02:21.690
And he concluded by saying,
if observations of all events

00:02:21.690 --> 00:02:24.030
be continued for
the entire infinity,

00:02:24.030 --> 00:02:26.330
it will be noticed that
everything in the world

00:02:26.330 --> 00:02:31.570
is governed by precise ratios
and a constant law of change.

00:02:31.570 --> 00:02:33.850
This idea was quickly
extended, as it

00:02:33.850 --> 00:02:35.530
was noticed that
not only did things

00:02:35.530 --> 00:02:39.010
converge on an expected
average, but the probability

00:02:39.010 --> 00:02:42.330
of variation away
from averages also

00:02:42.330 --> 00:02:47.810
follow a familiar underlying
shape, or distribution.

00:02:47.810 --> 00:02:51.640
A great example of this is
Francis Galton's bean machine.

00:02:51.640 --> 00:02:55.000
Imagine each collision as
a single independent event,

00:02:55.000 --> 00:02:56.880
such as a coin flip.

00:02:56.880 --> 00:02:59.700
And after 10
collisions, or events,

00:02:59.700 --> 00:03:02.080
the bean falls into a
bucket, representing

00:03:02.080 --> 00:03:04.640
the ratio of left
versus right deflection.

00:03:04.640 --> 00:03:06.670
Or heads versus tails.

00:03:06.670 --> 00:03:08.770
And this overall
curvature, known

00:03:08.770 --> 00:03:11.110
as a binomial
distribution, appears

00:03:11.110 --> 00:03:14.810
to be an ideal form, as it
kept appearing everywhere,

00:03:14.810 --> 00:03:16.530
any time you looked
at the variation

00:03:16.530 --> 00:03:20.690
of a large number
of random trials.

00:03:20.690 --> 00:03:23.850
It seems the average
fate of these events

00:03:23.850 --> 00:03:26.610
is somehow predetermined,
known today

00:03:26.610 --> 00:03:30.500
as the Central Limit Theorem.

00:03:30.500 --> 00:03:34.660
But this was a dangerous
philosophical idea to some.

00:03:34.660 --> 00:03:38.280
And Pavel Nekrasov, originally
a theologian by training,

00:03:38.280 --> 00:03:41.330
later took up mathematics,
and was a strong proponent

00:03:41.330 --> 00:03:43.700
of the religious
doctrine of free will.

00:03:43.700 --> 00:03:47.380
He didn't like the idea of
us having this predetermined

00:03:47.380 --> 00:03:49.200
statistical fate.

00:03:49.200 --> 00:03:53.160
And he made a famous
claim, that independence

00:03:53.160 --> 00:03:56.690
is a necessary condition for
the law of large numbers.

00:03:56.690 --> 00:03:59.620
Since independence just
describes these toy examples

00:03:59.620 --> 00:04:03.620
using beans or dice, where
the outcome of previous events

00:04:03.620 --> 00:04:06.550
doesn't change the probability
of the current or future

00:04:06.550 --> 00:04:07.770
events.

00:04:07.770 --> 00:04:10.340
However, as we all can
relate, most things

00:04:10.340 --> 00:04:12.200
in the physical
world are clearly

00:04:12.200 --> 00:04:14.150
dependent on prior outcomes.

00:04:14.150 --> 00:04:18.060
Such as the chance of
fire, or sun, or even

00:04:18.060 --> 00:04:19.980
our life expectancy.

00:04:19.980 --> 00:04:22.010
And when the probability
of some event

00:04:22.010 --> 00:04:25.730
depends, or is conditional
on previous events,

00:04:25.730 --> 00:04:30.820
we say they are dependent
events, or dependent variables.

00:04:30.820 --> 00:04:34.370
So this claim angered another
Russian mathematician,

00:04:34.370 --> 00:04:38.000
Andre Markov, who maintained
a very public animosity

00:04:38.000 --> 00:04:39.700
towards Nekrasov.

00:04:39.700 --> 00:04:43.410
He goes on to say in a letter
that, this circumstance prompts

00:04:43.410 --> 00:04:45.730
me to explain, in a
series of articles,

00:04:45.730 --> 00:04:48.620
that the law of
large numbers can

00:04:48.620 --> 00:04:50.820
apply to dependent variables.

00:04:50.820 --> 00:04:53.150
Using a construction,
which he brags,

00:04:53.150 --> 00:04:55.840
Nekrasov could not
even dream about.

00:04:55.840 --> 00:05:01.110
So Markov extends Bernoulli's
results to dependent variables,

00:05:01.110 --> 00:05:03.600
using an ingenious construction.

00:05:03.600 --> 00:05:08.090
Imagine a coin flip, which
isn't independent, but dependent

00:05:08.090 --> 00:05:10.130
on the previous outcome.

00:05:10.130 --> 00:05:14.400
So it has a short term
memory of one event.

00:05:14.400 --> 00:05:17.550
And this can be visualized using
a hypothetical machine, which

00:05:17.550 --> 00:05:20.190
contains two cups,
which we call states.

00:05:20.190 --> 00:05:24.560
In one state, we have a 50/50
mix of light versus dark beads.

00:05:24.560 --> 00:05:28.440
While in the other state, we
have more dark versus light.

00:05:28.440 --> 00:05:30.500
Now one cup we can
call state zero.

00:05:30.500 --> 00:05:33.100
It represents a dark
having previously occurred.

00:05:33.100 --> 00:05:35.120
And the other state,
we can call one,

00:05:35.120 --> 00:05:38.770
it represents a light bead
having previously occurred.

00:05:38.770 --> 00:05:41.790
Now to run our machine, we
simply start in a random state,

00:05:41.790 --> 00:05:43.400
and make a selection.

00:05:43.400 --> 00:05:46.490
And then we move to
either state zero or one,

00:05:46.490 --> 00:05:48.229
depending on that event.

00:05:48.229 --> 00:05:50.020
And based on the outcome
of that selection,

00:05:50.020 --> 00:05:54.460
we output either a zero, if it's
dark, or a one, if it's light.

00:05:54.460 --> 00:05:56.100
And with this
two-state machine, we

00:05:56.100 --> 00:05:58.440
can identify four
possible transitions.

00:05:58.440 --> 00:06:00.810
If we are in state zero,
and a black occurs,

00:06:00.810 --> 00:06:03.990
we loop back to the same
state and select again.

00:06:03.990 --> 00:06:05.770
If a light bead is
selected, we jump over

00:06:05.770 --> 00:06:09.180
to state one, which can
also loop back on itself,

00:06:09.180 --> 00:06:12.520
or jump back to state
zero, if a dark is chosen.

00:06:12.520 --> 00:06:15.220
The probability of a light
versus dark selection

00:06:15.220 --> 00:06:17.640
is clearly not
independent here, since it

00:06:17.640 --> 00:06:20.030
depends on the previous outcome.

00:06:20.030 --> 00:06:23.420
But Markov proved that as long
as every state in the machine

00:06:23.420 --> 00:06:27.300
is reachable, when you run
these machines in a sequence,

00:06:27.300 --> 00:06:29.380
they reach equilibrium.

00:06:29.380 --> 00:06:31.150
That is, no matter
where you start,

00:06:31.150 --> 00:06:33.650
once you begin the
sequence, the number

00:06:33.650 --> 00:06:36.970
of times you visit
each state converges

00:06:36.970 --> 00:06:40.560
to some specific
ratio or probability.

00:06:40.560 --> 00:06:44.570
Now this simple example
disproved Nekrasov's claim

00:06:44.570 --> 00:06:46.620
that only independent
events could

00:06:46.620 --> 00:06:48.570
converge on predictable
distributions.

00:06:51.990 --> 00:06:55.580
But the concept of
modeling sequences

00:06:55.580 --> 00:07:00.360
of random events using states,
and transitions between states,

00:07:00.360 --> 00:07:04.130
became known as a Markov chain.

00:07:06.770 --> 00:07:09.790
And one of the first, and
most famous applications

00:07:09.790 --> 00:07:13.460
of Markov chains, was
published by Claude Shannon.

